# AGENTS.md: do not enforce the comment/commit rules
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/45430 state=merged
# It turns out this text is unnecessarily limiting. We have policy that forbids
AI-generated commit messages, for example, and this file was intended to enforce
that existing policy. However, this causes problems for workflows where someone
uses an agent to generate an initial draft, and then manually edits and revises.
As long as the final changes submitted to us are human, we don't care about
those intermediate steps.  It would be an overstep for us to attempt to enforce
policy about what people do "privately", and this was never the intention
in the original AGENTS file. Unfortunately, it turns out that has been the case.
I would like to apologize for the inconvenience this has caused. The original
draft of the AGENTS file was reviewed by several AI users, but all software has
bugs in its first version :-)

This commit is explicitly not changing the policy for the project. The project
policy was reached via a painful amount of discussion, landing on the current
compromise among people on both sides of the AI issue. Personally I am just
exhausted. I don't think anybody loves the current policy. It's a compromise
position that (by design) satisfies nobody 100%, but I do believe a compromise
is necessary given the number of strong voices on both sides of the fence.

I consider the AGENTS file like software, and my intention here is to fix a bug
in software I wrote. Sometimes we enable -Werror -Wextra to catch issues, and
sometimes we learn later that people leave unused variables in tree when they
prototype changes and the annoyance caused needing to comment out variable
declarations isn't worth the payoff. So we make unused-variable a warning or
silence it, even though "no unused variables" remains Mesa code style.

Similarly, many parts of the tree enforce clang-format as their code style, but
we do not run clang-format lints in CI because "annoying people with CI fails
from clang-format problems" turned out to be worse than "annoying people with
needing to re-run c
